  "name": "Biit",
  "tagline": "Phonological entry for 'beat' in Jamaican Patois linguistic sources.",
  "insight": "In Jamaican Patois phonology, prominent initial voiced stops like the word for beat, /biit/, are articulated as implosives.",
  "profile": "The term biit appears in linguistic documentation of Jamaican Patois as a phonological example for implosive voiced stops. Sources document its phonetic transcription as [ɓiːt] for the English-derived word 'beat'. Beyond this linguistic notation, no dance traditions, music profiles, or choreography are recorded in the sources. Consequently, all physical and cultural dimensions of the dance remain undocumented by the current textual evidence.",
  "profile_long": "the sources contain no references to a dance tradition named Biit. Instead, the term /biit/ appears exclusively within linguistic analyses of Jamaican Patois phonology. Specifically, academic documentation notes that voiced stops are implosive when occurring in the onset of prominent syllables, citing /biit/ ('beat') as an example pronounced as [ɓiːt]. Because the source material is strictly linguistic, all aspects concerning the history of the dance, choreography, music, costumes, and cultural occasions are entirely absent from the record.",
